\section{Methods}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{GMT change and fire PM2.5 exposure}
\label{sec:method_regression}

To relate fire-attributable PM$_{2.5}$ exposure to global mean temperature (GMT) change, we estimate a country-level fixed effects regression for each country $c$:
\begin{align}
\label{eq:regression}
\bar{PM}_{ctsm}
=
\beta_c T_{ts}
+
\alpha_{cm}
+
\varepsilon_{ctsm}, 
\end{align}

where $\bar{PM}_{ctsm}$ is population-weighted\footnote{See Section \ref{sec:app_pop_weight} for population-weighting detail.} per-capita fire PM${2.5}$ exposure ($\mu$g/m$^3$/yr) for country $c$, time period $t$, climate scenario $s$, and fire model $m$; $T_{ts}$ is the GMT anomaly relative to the 1850–1900 pre-industrial baseline ($^\circ$C); $\alpha_{cm}$ is a country-fire-model-specific intercept; and $\varepsilon_{ctsm}$ is a residual. The coefficient of interest, $\beta_c$, captures the change in population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure associated with a 1°C increase in GMT for country $c$.  We assume that this relationship is linear and constant over time. 
{\color{red}
[justify/elaborate assumption?]
}


The dependent variable is constructed by combining three sources of gridded fire PM$_{2.5}$ data, each paired with its corresponding GMT value. \textcite{park_attributing_2024} provide historical decadal mean fire PM$_{2.5}$ for the 1960s through 2010s under three fire models (CLASSIC, JULES, SSiB4), yielding 18 observations per country (3 models $\times$ 6 decades). \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} provide projections for a baseline period (2001–2010) and two future periods (2041-2050, 2091-2100) under RCP4.5 and RCP8.5 using the CESM fire model, contributing 5 observations. \textcite{zhao_global_2025} provide two additional projections for 2095-2099 under SSP2-4.5 and SSP5-8.5, for a total of 25 observations per country. These gridded fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure data are aggregated to population-weighted per-capita country-level exposures.

The five fire models differ systematically in their absolute estimates of
PM$_{2.5}$ exposure---differences that reflect model structure rather than the
GMT-fire relationship of interest. A regression that does not account for these
differences would misattribute to GMT the variation in exposure levels driven by
fire model. We address this by including fire-model fixed effects $\alpha_{cm}$,
which absorb model-specific mean exposure differences so that $\beta_c$ is
identified from within-model covariation between GMT and fire PM$_{2.5}$ across
periods and scenarios\footnote{Figure \ref{fig:lof} in the Appendix illustrates
this for select countries.}. This specification assumes that the slope $\beta_c$
is constant across fire models: that is, all five models imply the same
sensitivity of fire PM$_{2.5}$ to warming, and differ only in their mean exposure levels. If fire models disagree on the slope as well as the intercept, the estimated $\beta_c$ represents an average across models, and the regression standard errors do not reflect how much $\beta_c$ would differ under an alternative fire model \emph{(See Section \ref{sec:method_uncertainty})}.

The resulting $\beta_c$ estimate is used directly in the GIVE integrated assessment model as the country-level damage function parameter linking GMT change to fire PM${2.5}$ exposure and, through a concentration-response coefficient, to associated mortality. Across 175 countries, $\beta_c$ is positive for 86\% of countries (median = 0.18 $\mu$g/m$^3$/yr per $^\circ$C), indicating that warming broadly increases population exposure to fire PM$_{2.5}$. Estimates are statistically significant at the 5\% level for 60\% of countries. Spatially, the largest responses are concentrated in northern North and South America, northern Asia, central Africa, and parts of Southeast Asia, while a minority of countries---predominantly in sub-Saharan Africa---exhibit negative $\beta_c$ \emph{(see Figure \ref{fig:beta_hist_map})}. {\color{red}[revise spatial description]}


