\subsection{GIVE fire PM2.5 Damage Function}

Our GIVE damage function takes a linear form analogous to the temperature-related mortality function applied in. \textcite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}.  

We translate warming-induced fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure into monetized mortality damages using a damage function that mirrors the structure of the temperature-related mortality component already embedded in GIVE \autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}. For country $c$ at time $t$, monetized damages are
\begin{align}
C_{ct} = V_{ct} \cdot M_{ct} \cdot \gamma \cdot \beta_c \cdot T_t,
\end{align}

where $V_{ct}$ is the value of a statistical life (VSL) in USD, $M_{ct}$ is the baseline all-cause mortality count, $\gamma = 0.008$ is the concentration-response coefficient (the proportional increase in mortality risk per 1~$\mu$g/m$^3$ increase in long-term PM$_{2.5}$ exposure), $\beta_c$ is the country-specific regression coefficient estimated in equation~(1) (the change in population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure per 1\textdegree C increase in GMT), and $T_t$ is the GMT anomaly relative to the pre-industrial baseline (\textdegree C).

The product $M_{ct} \cdot \gamma \cdot \beta_c \cdot T_t$ gives the number of excess deaths attributable to warming-induced fire PM$_{2.5}$ in country $c$ at time $t$. Multiplying by $V_{ct}$ converts these excess deaths to monetary damages. The VSL is sourced from \autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}: a baseline U.S. VSL for 2020 is adjusted for each country and year using GDP per capita with an income elasticity of one. The baseline all-cause mortality count, $M_{ct}$, is likewise sourced from \autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}, which projects these values for country and year from 2025 to 2300. 
