\label{sec:method_uncertainty}
\subsection{Uncertainty}

Following \textcite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}, we assess uncertainty in our
SC-CO$_2$ estimates via Monte Carlo simulation. In GIVE, each simulation draws from uncertain
socioeconomic and emissions trajectories (RFF-SPs), climate model parameters (FaIR),
sea-level model parameters (BRICK), and damage function coefficients
\autocite{rennert_comprehensive_2022}. To the damage function uncertainty already
present in GIVE, we add sampling over the two parameters specific to our fire
PM$_{2.5}$ component: the concentration-response coefficient $\gamma$ and the
country-level GMT-exposure coefficient $\beta_c$.

For $\gamma$, we draw from a normal distribution with mean $\bar{\gamma} = 0.008$ and standard error $SE(\gamma)= 0.00128$ derived from the 95\% confidence interval of the \textcite{pope_fine_2020} hazard
ratio (HR $= 1.08$; 95\%~CI: $1.06$--$1.11$ per 10~$\mu$g/m$^3$), converted to
per-unit exposure as $\gamma = (\text{HR} - 1)/10$ \footnote{We have: 
$    SE(\gamma)
    = \frac{\gamma_{\text{upper}} - \gamma_{\text{lower}}}{2 \times 1.96}
    = \frac{0.011 - 0.006}{3.92}
    \approx 0.00128
$. 
}. For $\beta_c$, we draw
independently for each country from a normal distribution with mean $\hat{\beta}_c$
and standard error $\widehat{SE}(\hat{\beta}_c)$ from the country-level fixed-effects
regression in equation  \ref{eq:regression}.

Several sources of uncertainty are not captured by this approach. We apply the
\textcite{pope_fine_2020} concentration-response estimate globally, abstracting from
regional heterogeneity in population susceptibility and background pollution levels.
As noted in Section \ref{sec:method_regression}, the regression standard errors underlying
our $\beta_c$ draws do not reflect how much $\beta_c$ would differ under an
alternative fire model; this between-model slope variation is absent from our Monte Carlo confidence intervals. Finally, we treat the linear functional form linking GMT to fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure as known, so uncertainty about potential nonlinearities in this relationship is not reflected in our confidence intervals. These omissions likely cause us to understate the true uncertainty in our SC-CO$_2$ estimates.

