\section{Appendix: Data}

\subsection{Fire PM2.5 Source Data and Regridding}

\textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}
\\
Generated global model output of fire emissions and associated PM$_{2.5}$ and O$_3$ fields under multiple future climate and population scenarios, with global coverage and particular emphasis on the United States. The dataset characterizes how climate driven changes in fire activity influence PM$_{2.5}$ concentrations relative to trends in anthropogenic emissions.
\\
\textcite{park_attributing_2024}
\\
Produced gridded global fire PM$_{2.5}$ fields spanning the 1960s through the 2010s by coupling three fire–vegetation models under both factual and counterfactual climate conditions and routing the resulting emissions through a chemical transport model. The PM$_{2.5}$ outputs are integrated with population exposure and baseline mortality data within a health risk framework to attribute fire-related PM$_{2.5}$ impacts to climate change.
\\
\textcite{zhao_global_2025}
\\
Constructed projected global wildfire emission datasets using an machine learning model of burned area, spanning the historical period through 2095–2099 under multiple Shared Socio-economic Pathway (SSP) scenarios. The emissions were translated into fire PM$_{2.5}$ concentration fields and aerosol radiative forcing estimates, forming the basis for downstream assessments of health and climate impacts.

We regrid PM$_{2.5}$ data from these multiple sources and native resolutions onto a unified $0.5^\circ \times 0.5^\circ$ global grid ($720 \times 360$), and subsequently merge the gridded fields with population data using longitude–latitude matching. The only difference across the three scripts lies in the resampling method: the \textcite{park_attributing_2024} dataset is interpolated using bilinear interpolation, the \textcite{pierce_estimating_2017} dataset is remapped using nearest-neighbor matching, and the \textcite{zhao_global_2025} dataset is aggregated using area-weighted averaging. All other processing steps are identical, including the definition of the target grid, coordinate harmonization, and the final left join with population data based on exact longitude–latitude pairs. The resulting grid is defined at the center of each $0.5^\circ$ cell, with longitude spanning $-179.75^\circ$ to $179.75^\circ$ and latitude spanning $-89.75^\circ$ to $89.75^\circ$, at $0.5^\circ$ intervals.

