\subsection{Appendix: Population Weighting}
\label{sec:app_pop_weight}

For grid cell $i$ in country $c$, let $PM_{itsm}$ denote fire
PM$_{2.5}$ exposure ($\mu$g/m$^3$/yr) under time period $t$, scenario
$s$, and fire model $m$. Each observation is already a period average:
a decade mean for \textcite{park_attributing_2024} and
\textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}, and a five-year mean for
\textcite{zhao_global_2025}.

Baseline population $\bar{pop}_{ic}$ is the average annual population
in cell $i$ of country $c$ over 2001-2010, sourced from
\textcite{pierce_estimating_2017}. Population is held fixed at this
baseline across all periods and scenarios to isolate changes in fire
PM$_{2.5}$ concentrations. Country-level baseline population is
$\bar{pop}_c = \sum_i \bar{pop}_{ic}$.

Population-weighted per-capita fire PM$_{2.5}$ exposure for country
$c$, period $t$, scenario $s$, and fire model $m$ is:
\begin{align}
\bar{PM}_{ctsm} = \frac{\sum_i PM_{itsm} \cdot \bar{pop}_{ic}}{\bar{pop}_c}, \nonumber
\end{align}
which is the dependent variable in equation~\eqref{eq:regression}.

