\section{Appendix: Regression Results}

Figure \ref{fig:lof} plots per-capita fire PM${2.5}$ exposure against GMT anomaly for twelve large-population countries, separating those where warming amplifies fire smoke exposure ($\beta_c > 0$, rows 1-2) from those where it attenuates it ($\beta_c < 0$, rows 3-4). Two features stand out. First, Russia exhibits a markedly larger GMT sensitivity than all other countries in the sample ($\beta_c = 1.73 \pm 0.49\ \mu$g/m$^3$/yr per $^\circ$C), with the fire-model lines diverging visibly across the observed temperature range; the remaining positive-$\beta_c$ countries show substantially smaller and more uniform slopes (0.15--0.28 $\mu$g/m$^3$/yr per $^\circ$C). Second, in the negative-$\beta_c$ panels the standard errors are large relative to the point estimates --- for Argentina, Ghana, and Australia the confidence intervals comfortably span zero --- indicating that the negative GMT-fire PM${2.5}$ relationship is imprecisely estimated in these regions and should be interpreted cautiously. 

Across all panels, the vertical spread between the five parallel model lines reveals that fire-model choice affects absolute exposure levels substantially, often by more than the GMT-driven variation captured by $\beta_c$ itself. {\color{red} More to say here? }

